insert into comment_summaries (post_id, model, summary, themes, sentiment_breakdown, audience_signals, content_requests, comment_count) values
  ('121b1576-9f9d-427b-8bf2-d812d6af806d', 'claude-opus-5', 'This is the channel''s clearest case of reach earned by controversy rather than enjoyment. The clip shows a cast member in revealing clothing in a hotel restaurant and on a hike in Madagascar, and roughly seven in ten comments are critical. The dominant charge is กาลเทศะ — wrong outfit for the time and place — followed by a strong national-representation argument that foreigners will generalise this to Thai women, and by genuine safety concern about a woman dressed that way in an unfamiliar country. A hotel-industry commenter explains dress expectations in shared dining rooms; another lists countries where the outfit would draw police attention. Several commenters praise the friend who said it plainly on camera and criticise the group for publishing the clip rather than warning her. A minority defend it as her right or find it funny, and a few fans openly criticise other fans for laughing. One highly-liked comment diagnoses the funnel out loud: post the clip, get criticised, get defended, and the argument itself drives views to the full episode.', array['[object Object]','[object Object]','[object Object]','[object Object]','[object Object]','[object Object]']::text[], '{"negative":61,"mixed":20,"positive":11,"neutral":8}'::jsonb, array['High reach is not evidence of brand health — this is the fifth most-viewed clip on the channel and the sentiment is majority negative','The Shorts audience for this clip is markedly older and more conservative than the channel''s core, and frames behaviour in terms of national representation','Viewers hold the group to a duty-of-care standard: they expect friends on camera to stop each other, and they blame the edit for publishing it','Brand-safety exposure — any sponsor attached to a clip in this pattern inherits the argument in the comments','Part of the fanbase polices the other part; defenders are called out by name, which raises the reputational cost of leaving criticism unanswered','Audience is media-literate about engagement mechanics and will say so publicly, which erodes trust faster than the original complaint']::text[], array['Respect local culture and dress codes when filming abroad','Do not cut Shorts in a way that strips the context of the full episode','Post a clarification if the scene was staged with hotel staff','Choose clips for Shorts with the out-of-context viewer in mind']::text[], 58),
  ('d488d2f1-4143-4c29-8fb1-c7102f363f79', 'claude-opus-5', 'The comment section reads as a confessional, not a music review. Viewers treat จางหาย as autobiography and respond by posting their own long-form breakup stories — several of the most-liked comments run 80-200 words about an ex they never got over, one about a partner lost to cancer. The second-largest cluster is career pride: viewers list everything Goy did herself (wrote lyrics and melody, sang, directed the MV) and frame her as an all-round talent rather than an actress who released a song. A third cluster reads the MV as a coded message to her real ex-partner Nicky, decoding specific shots (the football scene, the first book) and openly asking the pair to reunite. Lyric-quoting and timestamp comments show repeat listening. A visible minority arrived from the TV show The Wall Song rather than from the channel itself.', array['[object Object]','[object Object]','[object Object]','[object Object]','[object Object]']::text[], '{"positive":72,"mixed":16,"neutral":9,"negative":3}'::jsonb, array['Parasocial investment in Goy''s real-life relationship is the single strongest engagement driver — viewers know the ex by name and read shots as evidence','The comment section functions as a safe space for personal confession; the most-liked comments are long first-person stories, not compliments','Long-tenure fanbase that tracks her across acting, stage, writing and YouTube — repeated ''ภูมิใจในตัวพี่ก้อย'' framing signals ownership, not casual viewing','Terrestrial TV exposure (The Wall Song) imports viewers who did not know the song was hers — a proven top-of-funnel outside YouTube','Audience is protective: several comments ask her to prioritise self-love over the reunion narrative other fans push']::text[], array['A live performance of the song / hear her sing it at a concert','The duet version with Hai from The Wall Song released as its own track','A reaction video from the Friendsfly friend group, especially Vee','More original songs written by her']::text[], 141),
  ('ab56a446-39da-4030-8437-318543a5fa62', 'claude-opus-5', 'This MV is written from a fan''s point of view toward their idol, and the comment section is dominated by one specific fandom: Big D2B, the singer who died in 2007. Commenters recognise the ending photograph, the paper-star folding scene and the car-crash news clip as their own history, and many describe still caring for Big''s parents two decades later. The second cluster is fandom self-defence — viewers say being someone''s fan kept them alive or gave them a reason to work harder, and thank Goy for making that visible to outsiders. A third, much newer cluster arrived after Goy publicly revealed her relationship with Pita Limjaroenrat on 14 February 2026: dozens of comments say they came back to re-listen and now read the lyrics as being about him. A smaller cluster addresses living artists directly, asking them to look after their health.', array['[object Object]','[object Object]','[object Object]','[object Object]','[object Object]','[object Object]']::text[], '{"positive":66,"mixed":26,"neutral":6,"negative":2}'::jsonb, array['Cross-fandom acquisition: the song imported a 20-year-old fandom (Big D2B / RS-era) that has no prior relationship with the channel — an audience segment far older than the core','Multi-generational reach is explicit — several commenters say they became fans at 12 and are now over 30','News events drive measurable re-listening: the 14 Feb 2026 relationship announcement produced a distinct wave of ''came back to listen again'' comments on a song released earlier','The fan-to-idol POV is portable — commenters swapped in their own lost idols (Jonghyun, Moonbin), meaning the concept travels beyond one fandom','Audience frames Goy as an artist with a discography now, not a YouTuber who released a single — they compare this track to จางหาย unprompted']::text[], array['A version featuring Pita, half-joking but repeated many times','A proper feat. collaboration with Riu, the MV''s lead','More original songs — the audience now expects a release cadence','Push the track to 100M views / a follow-up in the same fan-POV concept']::text[], 108),
  ('5ab15c90-2a5f-48e7-95a4-533e0df0df5b', 'claude-opus-5', 'The dominant reaction is that the trio should stop being YouTubers-who-sang and formally debut as a girl group. Commenters assign K-pop positions unprompted (Goy as leader/rapper, Natty as lead vocal/center, Dream as maknae/visual), demand a T-Pop Stage appearance and say they will vote, and ask for a full album and a concert. The second cluster is surprise at production quality — styling, locations, colour grading, editing and UrboyTJ''s beat are repeatedly named, with many saying it exceeded what they expected from a channel side-project. This is also by far the most international comment section on the channel, with comments in English, Spanish, Portuguese, Chinese and Filipino arriving via UrboyTJ, Violette Wautier, BOSS KUNO and the Chinese survival show Chuang. A high-liked accessibility request for Thai subtitles from a hearing-impaired viewer sits unanswered. The Free Fire MAX sponsorship is pinned in the thread and drew no backlash at all.', array['[object Object]','[object Object]','[object Object]','[object Object]','[object Object]','[object Object]','[object Object]']::text[], '{"positive":91,"neutral":6,"mixed":2,"negative":1}'::jsonb, array['The largest non-Thai audience share of any post on the channel — music is the only format that exports internationally','Viewers apply K-pop idol frameworks unprompted (positions, debut, stage shows, voting), meaning they want an idol product rather than a YouTube single','Collaborator fandoms are a measurable acquisition channel — UrboyTJ, Violette Wautier, BOSS KUNO and the Chinese show Chuang each brought identifiable commenters','Sponsored integration is frictionless here: the pinned Free Fire MAX download link drew zero negative comments, unlike typical tie-in reactions','An unmet accessibility need is visible and highly upvoted — a hearing-impaired viewer asking for Thai captions','Fans self-organise view-count campaigns (''จะคอยปั่นวิวให้เรื่อยๆ'', ''10M ไม่เกินเอื้อม''), so release-day mobilisation is available if asked for']::text[], array['Appear on T-Pop Stage — repeated many times, with offers to vote','A full album and a live concert','A dance challenge version with guests from the talk show','Thai subtitles / closed captions for hearing-impaired viewers','Put the track on Line Melody for ringtones','A behind-the-scenes video of the shoot']::text[], 73),
  ('936c08a6-0f7c-4572-9470-82eb7c4eca17', 'claude-opus-5', 'A period-drama MV that produced the most analytical comment section on the channel. The largest cluster is close reading: viewers debate whether the title means ''choose me over her'' or ''choose me before yourself'', translate the English lyrics to settle it, and land on a self-love reading. One heavily-liked comment runs several hundred words dating the setting to the Rama VI era from the costumes and arguing the piece is about women having no agency under patriarchy rather than about a villain. The second cluster is Natty''s performance — her Thai classical dance converted viewers who say they were previously indifferent to her — and the third is a demand to expand this into a full film or series. Casting Jes as the husband is treated as a running joke about his lakorn typecasting, and his own fandom shows up in English. A recurring thread says Goy now has an authorial signature and predicts she will end up producing or directing.', array['[object Object]','[object Object]','[object Object]','[object Object]','[object Object]','[object Object]']::text[], '{"positive":87,"mixed":10,"neutral":3,"negative":0}'::jsonb, array['This audience will do close textual analysis when given something to analyse — several comments run hundreds of words on period accuracy and gender politics, which no other format on the channel produces','Individual member skills convert indifferent viewers: Natty''s dance is credited with turning casual watchers into her fans, so member-led showcases have measurable upside','Goy is now read as an author with a recognisable style rather than a YouTuber releasing songs — commenters compare across her whole discography unprompted','Her acting work cross-feeds the music: viewers connect the song to her lakorn role and treat the two catalogues as one body of work','Guest actors bring their fandoms, including non-Thai speakers who comment in English','The group''s mutual promotion is itself cited as a reason to support them — a soft asset worth keeping visible','The audience notices production detail down to a colour-grading jump at 3:06, so finish quality is being graded']::text[], array['Expand the MV into a full film, series or lakorn','More arrangements using Thai classical instruments','More acting and dance showcases for Natty','Let Goy play the lead in one of her own MVs','A collaboration with marr team']::text[], 53),
  ('0f32152a-a7b4-4aa6-9c63-40626cd167a8', 'claude-opus-5', 'A Short in which the group sings while running on a treadmill with guest Violette Wautier. The reaction splits cleanly: praise for Violette''s control as a trained singer, and genuine surprise at Dream, the member nobody expected to hold a steady tone. Surprise is the engagement mechanic here — the top comment thread is literally ''I wasn''t shocked by Vee because she''s a singer, I was shocked by Dream''. A large secondary cluster turns the comment section educational: viewers explain that treadmill singing is a real vocal-training method, citing Beyonce, K-pop trainees, army conscripts and their own singing teachers, and several add breath-support technique. A third cluster is self-deprecating relatability — viewers say they cannot even talk while walking. Almost no one asks for anything; this format converts to comments through recognition and knowledge-sharing rather than requests.', array['[object Object]','[object Object]','[object Object]','[object Object]','[object Object]']::text[], '{"positive":89,"neutral":10,"mixed":1,"negative":0}'::jsonb, array['A Short built on a verifiable demonstration of skill outperforms conversation-based clips — the hook needs no context and works for viewers who do not know the channel','Surprise is the mechanic: the strongest reaction is to the member the audience did NOT expect to perform well, not to the professional','The format invites the audience to contribute expertise (breath support, athlete-singer training), producing high comment volume at very low effort','The guest''s own fanbase arrives with her — several commenters say they have followed Violette since her singing competition, not from this channel','Members are followed individually, not only as a trio; per-member bias declarations are common and would support member-led content']::text[], array['More of the same treadmill/vocal-test format with other guests','A full-length version rather than a Short','Let Violette sing properly on the channel']::text[], 58),
  ('b2193385-53fe-4363-a5dc-0c1b24ef0c96', 'claude-opus-5', 'The most-viewed talk episode on the channel, and the comments show why: the guest brought his own organised fandom. Nicky Nachat''s followers self-identify as ''แก๊งขาหื่น'', announce which channel they came from, and run explicit view-count campaigns because the guest promised rewards at milestones — a boat trip at 2M, a Japan trip at 3M — with commenters reporting each threshold as it is passed. The second and equally large cluster is retroactive shipping: the pair later dated in real life, so viewers return to re-read the episode as evidence, timestamping blushes and physical contact, and a later wave returns again after the relationship ends, which gives a handful of openly sad comments. Rewatching is heavy and self-reported, ten or more times for many. A distinct cluster uses the comments as a guest-request board, naming Oat Pramote, Lee Thanat and Tor Thanapob and asking others to upvote.', array['[object Object]','[object Object]','[object Object]','[object Object]','[object Object]','[object Object]']::text[], '{"positive":77,"mixed":16,"neutral":5,"negative":2}'::jsonb, array['The guest''s own fandom is the acquisition engine for talk episodes — commenters state which channel they arrived from, giving a traceable referral path','Reward-gated view targets work: the guest''s public promise at 2M/3M/4M produced self-organised, self-reported view campaigns and is a repeatable growth mechanic','Real-life romance converted this episode into an evergreen asset — rewatch waves are keyed to later news events (dating reveal, EP100, breakup), so old episodes keep earning views','The comment section operates as a fandom home base with its own name and in-group identity, not just a reaction space','An explicit, upvote-ranked guest demand list already exists in the comments and can be read as a booking backlog','Chemistry, not the format, is what viewers rewatch — the questions are secondary to reading the hosts'' reactions']::text[], array['Book Oat Pramote, Lee Thanat and Tor Thanapob as guests','Bring Nicky onto ยังไงไหนเล่า as well','Honour the milestone promises — the boat trip and the Japan trip','A follow-up episode with the same guest']::text[], 53)
